<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/general-relativity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    General relativity (1915) is Einstein's answer to an awkward question: if special
    relativity says "simultaneous" depends on the observer, what does <em>gravity</em> do to
    that story? His answer redefines gravity out of existence: mass and energy
    <strong>curve spacetime</strong>, and objects simply follow the straightest available
    paths (geodesics) through the curved geometry. Planets don't feel a pull; they coast
    along dented spacetime. The school "space physics" syllabus — Earth, the Solar System,
    the Sun, stars, the Universe — reads, in this light, as a tour of gravitational
    regimes: from the gentle curvature you can measure with atomic clocks, through the
    Solar System's corrections to Newton, to stars that collapse past the point of no
    return and a Universe whose expansion is the same field equation on the largest stage.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="gravitational time dilation — clocks run slower deep in the curve; at the horizon (x = 1) they stop" widget="plotter" config={gravCfg} />

  <p>
    Time itself is the sensitive instrument. Near a one-solar-mass black hole's horizon, a
    clock ticks at nearly zero compared with a distant observer. In the weak gravity of
    Earth the effect is tiny — but real: your feet age slightly less than your head, and
    GPS satellites must correct both this (clocks run faster up there, +45 µs/day) and
    their speed (−7 µs/day) or navigation drifts kilometres per day.
  </p>

  <h2>The Earth: gravity as geometry you can clock</h2>
  <p>
    Start with the Earth (6.1.1) as a curved-spacetime laboratory. School gravity says
    g = 9.81 m/s², weight = mg; the relativistic refinement is that Earth's mass warps both
    space (negligibly, at this scale) and <em>time</em> (measurably): a clock at sea level
    runs slower than one on a mountain, in proportion to the gravitational potential. The
    effect is about one part in 10¹⁶ per metre of height — and modern optical clocks are
    exactly that good: NIST researchers resolved the rate difference between two clocks
    33 cm apart vertically (2010), turning height measurement into time measurement.
    Gravitational redshift — light climbing out of a potential well loses frequency — was
    confirmed terrestrially in the Harvard tower experiment (Pound–Rebka, 1959) across
    22.5 m. The equivalence principle underlies it all: in a freely falling box, gravity
    vanishes locally; the residual differences between nearby falling objects (tidal
    effects) are what curvature actually is. Weight, the first physics quantity a child
    meets, turns out to be geometry's most personal manifestation.
  </p>

  <h2>The Solar System: where Newton bends</h2>
  <p>
    The Solar System (6.1.2) is the arena where general relativity met its first test.
    Newton's gravity got the planets almost exactly right — almost. Mercury's perihelion
    (closest point to the Sun) drifts 43 arcseconds per century beyond every Newtonian
    correction, a discrepancy known since 1859. Einstein's 1915 calculation reproduced the
    43 arcseconds exactly, with no tuning — for days afterwards he was, by his own account,
    "beside himself with joyous excitement". The mechanism: near the Sun, spacetime curvature
    slightly exceeds what an inverse-square force predicts, so the elliptical orbit's axis
    precesses. The same mathematics runs the Global Positioning System's clocks (the
    45.7 µs/day gravitational gain and 7.2 µs/day kinematic loss noted above) and corrects
    signals bounced off Mars landers. Even the "dark matter" searches on the astronomy
    ladder are calibrated against Solar-System tests: any theory of gravity must first
    survive the Mercury-and-GPS gauntlet, and general relativity has passed every probe —
    Cassini's 2003 Shapiro-delay measurement confirmed the light-bending prediction to 2
    parts in 100,000.
  </p>

  <h2>The Sun as a star: mass, light and the bending of rays</h2>
  <p>
    The Sun (6.2.1) — 1.989 × 10³⁰ kg, the mass that sets the Solar System's geometry —
    provided the 1919 experiment that made Einstein famous. Light grazing the solar limb
    must bend by α = 4GM/c²R ≈ 1.75 arcseconds: twice the "Newtonian" value half of which
    even Einstein's 1911 theory had given. Eddington's eclipse expeditions (Príncipe and
    Sobral) photographed stars near the eclipsed Sun and found the shift — headline news
    worldwide, the first celebrity scientist. The deeper point: light bending is not
    "gravity pulling on light" but light travelling straight through curved space; the
    bending angle is geometry. The same equation, run in reverse, gives gravitational
    lensing — galaxy clusters as natural telescopes that have magnified the most distant
    galaxies ever observed — and the Shapiro delay, the extra milliseconds radar takes to
    cross the Sun's dented neighbourhood. The Sun also anchors the scale: convert its mass
    through the Schwarzschild radius formula R = 2GM/c² and you get 2.95 km — the size at
    which the Sun's mass would become a black hole. It is nowhere near; but the number
    hangs over every star's biography.
  </p>

  <h2>Stars: the lifecycle and the collapse endpoint</h2>
  <p>
    Stars (6.2.2) are general relativity's most dramatic territory. A star is a
    gravitational equilibrium: fusion pressure balances self-gravity, for ten billion years
    for the Sun. When the fuel ends, the balance fails, and the star's mass decides the
    fate. Below about 1.4 solar masses (the Chandrasekhar limit, 1931), electron degeneracy
    pressure holds the corpse up as a white dwarf — Earth-sized, teaspoon-tonne density.
    Above it, neutron degeneracy holds a neutron star — city-sized, spinning, the pulsars
    Jocelyn Bell found in 1967. Above roughly 20–25 solar masses initially, nothing holds:
    collapse passes the Schwarzschild radius and a black hole forms, a region where the
    geometry is so curved that every future-pointing path leads inward. The 2015 LIGO
    detection of gravitational waves (two black holes of 36 and 29 solar masses merging
    1.3 billion light-years away — Nobel 2017) was the first direct hearing of spacetime
    ringing, and the 2019 and 2022 Event Horizon Telescope images of the M87 and Milky Way
    central black holes made the shadows visible. The syllabus's "life cycle of a star" is,
    in expert terms, a catalogue of gravitational collapse outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    The binary pulsar gave the theory its most patient witness. The Hulse–Taylor
    binary pulsar PSR B1913+16 (discovered 1974, Nobel 1993) is two neutron stars
    whipping round each other every 7.75 hours; general relativity predicts their
    orbit must shrink — energy carried away as gravitational waves — by about 3.5
    metres per year, with a specific phase-by-phase curve. Four decades of timing
    measurements match the prediction to better than 0.2% — an indirect
    confirmation of gravitational waves twenty years before LIGO heard them
    directly, and still the cleanest long-baseline test of the theory's
    radiative sector.
  </p>

  <h2>The Universe: the field equation at full scale</h2>
  <p>
    The Universe (6.2.3) is where the theory meets cosmology. Apply Einstein's equation to
    a universe that is, at large scale, uniform and isotropic, and the geometry must
    expand or contract — the Friedmann solutions (1922–24), which Einstein initially
    resisted by inventing a cosmological constant he later called his biggest blunder.
    Hubble's 1929 observations settled it: the Universe expands. Run the expansion back and
    you reach a hot dense beginning — the Big Bang — whose relics (the cosmic microwave
    background, light-element abundances) the astronomy ladder's cosmology page treats in
    full. The relativistic point worth making here: cosmic expansion is not galaxies flying
    through space but space itself growing — the metric changing with time, exactly the
    curvature dynamics of the field equation, with no centre and no edge. Gravitational
    waves, black holes, cosmological expansion: three syllabus-level phenomena, one tensor
    equation — G = 8πT, "matter tells spacetime how to curve; spacetime tells matter how
    to move" (Wheeler).
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "gravity in general relativity is a force that attracts,
    just described differently." There is no force: a falling object is in <em>free
    fall</em>, feeling nothing, tracing the straightest line available in curved spacetime.
    What we call "the force of gravity" standing on the ground is the floor's resistance —
    the electromagnetic repulsion of the floor's atoms preventing you from following your
    geodesic. An astronaut on the ISS is not "beyond gravity" (Earth's gravity there is
    about 90% of surface strength); she is finally unresisted, coasting along her geodesic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "black holes are cosmic vacuum cleaners that will suck
    in the Solar System." Replace the Sun with a black hole of the same mass and Earth's
    orbit would not change: far from the horizon, the geometry is identical to any other
    one-solar-mass object's. Black holes curve spacetime intensely <em>near</em> the
    horizon; at a distance their gravity is ordinary. The danger zone is close approach,
    not the hole's mere existence — and the nearest known black hole is over a thousand
    light-years away.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the Universe is expanding into something, and it has a
    centre where the Big Bang happened." Expansion is an <em>internal</em> stretching of
    space: every galaxy recedes from every other, like raisins in a rising loaf or dots on
    an inflating balloon — the balloon's surface is all there is, with no edge and no
    outside point of view required. The Big Bang happened everywhere at once: it is the
    moment every line of sight, traced back, meets a hot dense beginning. "Where is the
    centre?" has the same status as "what is north of the North Pole?"
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The equation, honestly.</strong> Einstein's field equation is ten coupled
    nonlinear partial differential equations (packed into G<sub>μν</sub> = 8πG/c⁴ · T<sub>μν</sub>): the
    Einstein tensor G encodes spacetime's curvature, the stress-energy tensor T encodes
    matter's energy, momentum and pressure. Nonlinearity means gravity gravitates —
    solutions cannot be superposed, which is why the two-body problem has no exact closed
    solution and why gravitational-wave physics needed decades of numerical relativity
    (simulations that finally merged two black holes on a computer in 2005) before LIGO
    could match its detections to templates. Exact solutions are treasured: Schwarzschild
    (1916, the non-rotating hole), Kerr (1963, the rotating one — realistic, since angular
    momentum is conserved), Friedmann (cosmology), and gravitational waves themselves were
    only shown to be physically real (not coordinate artifacts) by Bondi and colleagues in
    the late 1950s.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Singularities and the quantum wall.</strong> The theory predicts its own
    breakdown: inside every black hole (and at the Big Bang) curvature grows without bound
    — a singularity, the theorems of Penrose (1965, Nobel 2020) and Hawking showing that
    collapse to singularity is generic, not a special-case curiosity. A "singularity" is
    physics-speak for "the theory has stopped applying"; the near-universal expectation is
    that quantum gravity takes over. Candidates — string theory, loop quantum gravity —
    remain experimentally untested; the regime where both general relativity and quantum
    mechanics matter (Planck scale, 10⁻³⁵ m) is a billion times beyond any collider.
    Meanwhile Hawking's black-hole radiation (1974, still undetected) poses the information
    paradox — do the quantum states falling in escape back out? — which is the sharpest
    clue anyone has about what the quantum theory of gravity must look like.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Tests still running.</strong> General relativity is not a museum piece; it is
    under active test. Pulsar timing arrays (NANOGrav's 2023 evidence for a low-frequency
    gravitational-wave background, likely from supermassive black hole pairs) probe a
    frequency band LIGO cannot hear. The Event Horizon Telescope sharpens toward movies of
    accretion flows. Gravitational-wave detections now number in the hundreds, each
    consistent with general relativity's waveform predictions — and each, in principle, a
    chance to catch a deviation. The cosmological constant, reinstated as dark energy, is
    measured to be the dominant component of the Universe and completely unexplained —
    the largest discrepancy between theory's possibility and theory's mechanism in
    physics. The lesson of the last century: general relativity wins every rematch; the
    open question is what it will meet in the next one.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Einstein (1915); Eddington (1919); LIGO/Virgo, PRL (2016); EHT, ApJL (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
